Lec 6
 
Parametric equations
 
Position vector: \vec{r}(t) = \langle x(t), y(t), z(t) \rangle
 
Cycloid (wheel radius 1, at unit speed)
 

\vec{r}(t) = \langle t - \sin t,\; 1 - \cos t \rangle

 
Velocity vector: \vec{v} = \frac{d\vec{r}}{dt} = \langle \frac{dx}{dt}, \frac{dy}{dt}, \frac{dz}{dt} \rangle

\vec{v} = \langle 1 - \cos t,\; \sin t \rangle

(At t=0: \vec{v} = 0!)
 
Speed (scalar):

|\vec{v}| = \sqrt{(1 - \cos t)^2 + \sin^2 t} = \sqrt{2 - 2\cos t}

 
Acceleration (vector): \vec{a} = \frac{d\vec{v}}{dt}

\vec{a} = \langle \sin t,\; \cos t \rangle

(At t=0: \vec{a} = \langle 0, 1 \rangle)


Arc length
 
s = \text{distance travelled along trajectory}

\left|\frac{ds}{dt}\right| = \text{speed} = |\vec{v}|

 
Example: Length of one arch of a cycloid:

\int_0^{2\pi} \sqrt{2 - 2\cos t}\, dt

 
 
 
Unit tangent vector
 

\hat{T} = \frac{\vec{v}}{|\vec{v}|}


\frac{d\vec{r}}{ds} = \hat{T}, \quad \frac{ds}{dt} = |\vec{v}|


\vec{v} = \frac{d\vec{r}}{dt} = \frac{d\vec{r}}{ds} \frac{ds}{dt} = \hat{T} \frac{ds}{dt}

 
Velocity has:
 
- Direction: Tangent to the trajectory (\hat{T})
​
- Length: Speed (\frac{ds}{dt})
 
 
 
Approximation over time \Delta t
 

\frac{\Delta s}{\Delta t} \approx \text{speed}, \quad \frac{\Delta \vec{r}}{\Delta t} \approx \hat{T} \frac{\Delta s}{\Delta t}

Taking the limit as \Delta t \to 0:

\frac{d\vec{r}}{dt} = \hat{T} \frac{ds}{dt}

 
 
 
Example: Kepler's second law (1609)
 
- Motion of planets lies in a plane.
​
- The area swept out by the line from the Sun to the planet is at a constant rate.
​
- Newton later explained this using the formula for gravitational attraction.
 
Kepler's law in vector terms
 
1. Area swept in time \Delta t:

\text{Area} \approx \frac12 |\vec{r} \times \Delta \vec{r}| \approx \frac12 |\vec{r} \times \vec{v}| \Delta t

​
2. The law states:

|\vec{r} \times \vec{v}| = \text{constant}

2) Plane of motion

The plane of motion contains \vec{r} and \vec{v}.
 
- The direction of \vec{r} \times \vec{v} is normal to the plane of motion.
​
- Kepler’s 2nd law \iff \vec{r} \times \vec{v} = \text{constant vector}

\iff \frac{d}{dt} \left(\vec{r} \times \vec{v}\right) = 0

 
 
 
Product rule for derivatives
 
The product rule applies to both dot and cross products:

\frac{d}{dt}\left(\vec{a} \cdot \vec{b}\right),\quad \frac{d}{dt}\left(\vec{a} \times \vec{b}\right)

 
Applying the product rule to \frac{d}{dt}\left(\vec{r} \times \vec{v}\right) = 0:

\frac{d\vec{r}}{dt} \times \vec{v} + \vec{r} \times \frac{d\vec{v}}{dt} = 0

Substitute \frac{d\vec{r}}{dt} = \vec{v} and \frac{d\vec{v}}{dt} = \vec{a}:

\vec{v} \times \vec{v} + \vec{r} \times \vec{a} = 0

Since \vec{v} \times \vec{v} = 0, this simplifies to:

\vec{r} \times \vec{a} = 0

This implies \vec{a} \parallel \vec{r} (the acceleration vector is parallel to the position vector).
 
 
 
Conclusion
 
Kepler’s 2nd law \iff \vec{a} \parallel \vec{r}
This corresponds to the gravitational force being parallel to \vec{r}.



