Partial Derivatives
 
Single-variable function review
 
- Function: f(x)
​
- Derivative:

f'(x) = \frac{df}{dx} = \lim_{\Delta x \to 0} \frac{f(x+\Delta x) - f(x)}{\Delta x}

​
- Geometric meaning: slope of the tangent line at x
​
- Approximation formula:

f(x) \approx f(x_0) + f'(x_0)(x - x_0)

 
Partial derivative definition
 
- Partial derivative with respect to x at (x_0, y_0):

\frac{\partial f}{\partial x}(x_0, y_0) = \lim_{\Delta x \to 0} \frac{f(x_0+\Delta x, y_0) - f(x_0, y_0)}{\Delta x}

​
- Partial derivative with respect to y at (x_0, y_0):

\frac{\partial f}{\partial y}(x_0, y_0) = \lim_{\Delta y \to 0} \frac{f(x_0, y_0+\Delta y) - f(x_0, y_0)}{\Delta y}

​
- Notation: \frac{\partial f}{\partial x} = f_x, \frac{\partial f}{\partial y} = f_y
 
Example
 
- Let f(x,y) = x^3y + y^2
​
- \frac{\partial f}{\partial x}: treat y as constant, x as variable

\frac{\partial f}{\partial x} = 3x^2y + 0 = 3x^2y

​
- \frac{\partial f}{\partial y}: treat x as constant, y as variable

\frac{\partial f}{\partial y} = x^3 + 2y